function _sfc_ssrRender(_ctx, _push, _parent, _attrs, $props, $setup, $data, $options) {
  _push(`<div${ssrRenderAttrs(_attrs)}><h1 id="week-06-—-bytes-to-instructions-starting-an-8086-decoder-in-c" tabindex="-1">Week 06 — Bytes to instructions: starting an 8086 decoder in C <a class="header-anchor" href="#week-06-—-bytes-to-instructions-starting-an-8086-decoder-in-c" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Week 06 — Bytes to instructions: starting an 8086 decoder in C&quot;">​</a></h1><p><strong>Status:</strong> Implemented and verified on 2026-09-22 with GCC and Clang in debug, optimized and sanitizer configurations (the reference also with <code>-Wconversion -Wsign-conversion</code>). The package contains learner and instructor materials, the C sources, an encoding-direction oracle, listing fixtures, the <code>objdump</code> cross-check, the stretch programs, tests and a Makefile. Start at <a href="/materials/week-06/README.html">the weekly guide</a>; the manual cross-check and execution results are in <a href="/materials/week-06/instructor/validation.html">validation.md</a>. The requirements below are the acceptance specification the implementing agent followed, in the same way <a href="/materials/week-04/PLAN.html">week 4&#39;s plan</a> governed <a href="/materials/week-04/README.html">the week-four package</a>. The corrections section records what implementation found; the original text is left in place so each change is visible.</p><p><strong>Parent plan:</strong> <a href="/materials/PLAN.html">52-week syllabus</a>, first week of the “Instructions, a small simulator, and reading assembly” section (weeks 6–12, checkpoint 2 in week 12). <strong>Time budget:</strong> 8–10 hours (core target 600 minutes; see the acceptance section). <strong>Audience:</strong> Programmers who completed weeks 1–5 or have equivalent C experience: bit operations and byte inspection (week 1), object representation and byte order (week 2), strict parsers with ordered error classes and bounded file reading (week 4), and a multi-file program with a documented contract (week 5). Week 5&#39;s closing question, “which machine instructions does the distance loop actually execute?”, motivates the section; this week learns to read instruction bytes on a machine small enough to decode completely.</p><h2 id="why-this-week-exists" tabindex="-1">Why this week exists <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-this-week-exists" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Why this week exists&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A C program becomes bytes that a processor decodes. Reading compiler output, which the course asks for from week 11 on, is much easier after decoding a real instruction set by hand and in code. The 8086 is used because its encoding is small, documented in one manual, and still visible in x86-64: the ModR/M byte, the register numbering and many opcodes survive. Week 6 starts a C decoder for a <strong>specified subset</strong>: <code>mov</code>, <code>add</code>, <code>sub</code> and <code>cmp</code> whose operands are registers or immediates. Memory operands and displacements (the other <code>mod</code> values of the ModR/M byte) are week 7; simulation is weeks 8–10. The C lessons are exact: reading bytes with unsigned types, extracting bit fields with shifts and masks, assembling little-endian values independently of the host&#39;s byte order, interpreting a two&#39;s-complement bit pattern without an implementation-defined conversion, and decoding a stream with bounds checks and explicit errors.</p><p>By the end, a learner should be able to split an opcode byte and a ModR/M byte into their fields and explain each field&#39;s role (<code>d</code>, <code>w</code>, <code>s</code>, <code>mod</code>, <code>reg</code>, <code>r/m</code>); name the 8- and 16-bit registers selected by a 3-bit field and explain why <code>ah</code> is not <code>sp</code>; hand-decode a byte sequence in the subset and predict its length before decoding its operands; write a decoder whose output is a function of the bytes alone, which rejects anything outside the subset with a stated status and offset; and explain why decoding is a function from bytes to text but assembling is not its inverse (one text can have several encodings).</p><p>Do not add later-week topics: memory operands, effective-address calculation, displacements or segment prefixes (week 7), a public decoder library API or shared library (week 7), jumps and loops beyond the optional stretch exercise (week 9), register or flag simulation (weeks 8–9), cycle estimates (week 11), or x86-64 decoding beyond the one comparison in P06. Handwritten assembly is not required this week; the course&#39;s short assembly probe is week 11.</p><h2 id="corrections-incorporated-after-implementation" tabindex="-1">Corrections incorporated after implementation <a class="header-anchor" href="#corrections-incorporated-after-implementation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Corrections incorporated after implementation&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>2026-09-22 plan review, before implementation:</strong> The normative requirements below incorporate these fixes. A <code>snprintf</code> directly into the caller&#39;s buffer can modify it before reporting that it is too small, so E05 formats into a local buffer and commits only after validation. E06 now distinguishes byte-decode failures from an output buffer that is too small and from file errors; it performs a sizing/validation pass before writing. A failed write to standard output can leave partial output, so the empty-output guarantee covers read/decode/format failures, with output failure reported separately. The independent encoding oracle needs explicit negative-case rules; absence from its generated table alone cannot establish the required error status. The optional table decoder needs complete immediate-domain comparison or a proof in addition to two-byte-prefix tests. The 1979 Intel manual&#39;s decoding guide lists <code>0x82</code> as an 8-bit group-one form; excluding it is solely this course&#39;s scope choice (<a href="http://www.bitsavers.org/components/intel/8086/9800722-03_The_8086_Family_Users_Manual_Oct79.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">manual</a>).</p><p><strong>2026-09-22, found during implementation</strong> (evidence in <a href="/materials/week-06/instructor/validation.html">validation.md</a>):</p><ul><li><p><strong>Manual tables and pages, recorded.</strong> Checked against the scanned manual, every entry of the course table agrees with it:</p><ul><li>Table 4-7 (single-bit fields), p. 4-19;</li><li>Tables 4-8 to 4-10 (MOD, REG and R/M fields), p. 4-20;</li><li>Table 4-12 (encoding): MOV p. 4-22, ADD p. 4-23, SUB and CMP p. 4-24;</li><li>Table 4-13 (decoding guide), pp. 4-27 to 4-33.</li></ul><p>Table 4-13 lists ADD, ADC, SBB, SUB and CMP for <code>0x82</code> and marks REG <code>001</code>, <code>100</code> and <code>110</code> (OR, AND, XOR) “(not used)” in <strong>both</strong> <code>0x82</code> and <code>0x83</code>. The subset uses only <code>000</code>, <code>101</code> and <code>111</code>, so nothing changes. The main bitsavers site refused a scripted download (403); the identical file was taken from its mirror <code>bitsavers.trailing-edge.com</code>.</p></li><li><p><strong>Where the E07 cases live.</strong> The plan names <code>tests/learner_cases.txt</code>, a shared directory, so the exemplar would have given the learner&#39;s answer away. Each package has its own <code>cases.txt</code> (<code>learner/cases.txt</code>, <code>instructor/cases.txt</code>), and the supplied runner is <a href="/source/week-06/tests/run_cases.py.html">tests/run_cases.py</a>. A case&#39;s expectation is checked against the oracle, which checks the hand derivation, and against the decoder.</p></li><li><p><strong>Usage errors exit 1.</strong> “All errors exit 1” is applied to wrong argument counts too (<code>decode8086: usage: decode8086 FILE</code>), rather than the conventional 2.</p></li><li><p><strong>The oracle&#39;s 16-bit sample must contain every value the exhaustive test can form.</strong> The exhaustive test appends the fixed bytes <code>34 12</code> after each two-byte prefix. For <code>mov r16, imm16</code> and the 16-bit accumulator forms, the immediate is then <code>0x34XX</code>, so all 256 such values are enumerated. The oracle raises <code>Unsampled</code> instead of guessing when a test uses an immediate it did not enumerate (the revised plan&#39;s negative-case rule).</p></li><li><p><strong>An equivalent mutant worth teaching.</strong> Reading an <code>s = 1</code> immediate as a signed byte gives the same value as sign-extending it and reading 16 bits. The mutation check cannot kill that change, and the answer key uses it to show that sign extension changes width, not value.</p></li><li><p><strong>P06 on this build.</strong> Week 5&#39;s <code>geo.o</code> at <code>-O2</code> contains no integer <code>0x89</code>/<code>0x8B</code> <code>mov</code>: its code is SSE2. The fallback fixture <code>48 89 D8</code> and the <code>-O0</code> prologue <code>48 89 E5</code> (<code>mov rbp, rsp</code>) are used, as the revised plan anticipated.</p></li></ul><h2 id="constraints-carried-forward-from-earlier-reviews" tabindex="-1">Constraints carried forward from earlier reviews <a class="header-anchor" href="#constraints-carried-forward-from-earlier-reviews" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Constraints carried forward from earlier reviews&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The week-2 and week-4 lessons still apply: verify every claim; state overflow contracts; state explicit limits (input size, buffer sizes, accepted values); validate before committing outputs; sequence side effects; give learners typed, warning-clean TODO functions and supplied drivers; mutation-test the harness and classify survivors. Fixed expected outputs are legitimate here, as they were for week 4&#39;s format: the instruction encoding is fully specified and platform-independent, so the decoder&#39;s text is compared exactly.</p><p>The <a href="/materials/week-03/actions.html">week-3 review</a> found defects that a passing suite had not revealed. Its five actions are generalized here and checked in the acceptance section:</p><ul><li><strong>Derive results from the original data, not from a lossy intermediate (A1).</strong> Immediate values are rebuilt from the original bytes and the <code>w</code> and <code>s</code> bits; the instruction&#39;s printed text is produced from the decoded structure, never by re-parsing earlier text; the reported error offset is the offset of the instruction&#39;s first byte in the original stream. Tests include inputs that expose a shortcut: an 8-bit immediate <code>0x80</code>–<code>0xFF</code> with <code>s = 1</code> (sign extension changes the value) and with <code>w = 0</code> (it does not).</li><li><strong>Check every proof step against a counterexample search (A2).</strong> The plan&#39;s structural claims — for example “an instruction&#39;s length is known once the bytes that select its form have been read” — are checked exhaustively over the subset&#39;s two-byte prefixes before any explanation relies on them. For example, “an instruction&#39;s length is a function of its first byte” holds for the instructions this subset <em>accepts</em>, but the <em>status</em> is not a function of the first byte (<code>0x80</code>–<code>0x83</code> and <code>0x88</code>–<code>0x8B</code> need the ModR/M byte to decide support), and the length claim fails as soon as memory modes are admitted (in week 7, <code>mod = 01</code> and <code>10</code> add displacement bytes). The answer key states exactly how far the claim holds, with the exhaustive check and the week-7 counterexample. A test illustrates a proof; it does not replace one.</li><li><strong>Keep exact claims exact (A3).</strong> Decoding is integer work and is compared exactly. Where the C standard makes a conversion implementation-defined (a <code>uint16_t</code> value above <code>INT16_MAX</code> converted to <code>int16_t</code>, C11 §6.3.1.3 ¶3) or leaves a result implementation-defined (right shift of a negative value, §6.5.7 ¶5), the reference code avoids it; the answer key names the clause rather than relying on what one compiler does.</li><li><strong>Use precise terms and demonstrate them (A4).</strong> Distinguish an <em>opcode</em> (the bits that select an operation and form) from an <em>instruction</em> (the whole encoded sequence); <em>decoding</em> (bytes to a structured instruction), <em>disassembling</em> (decoding plus printing text) and <em>assembling</em> (text to bytes, not unique); the 8086&#39;s architectural registers from x86-64&#39;s; and the ISA model from any microarchitecture (week 12). Each distinction is demonstrated with a concrete byte sequence, as week 3&#39;s revised E04.Q demonstrates a basis change instead of naming it.</li><li><strong>Keep the workload credible and the uncertainty explicit (A5).</strong> The exhaustive oracle and the <code>objdump</code> cross-check are <strong>supplied</strong>. The learner explains why each is independent of their decoder, traces one case through the oracle, and adds a small number of purposeful test cases; they do not write a second decoder or an encoder. The estimate below is unpiloted; the validation record says so and includes the pilot procedure.</li></ul><h2 id="required-readings-and-viewing" tabindex="-1">Required readings and viewing <a class="header-anchor" href="#required-readings-and-viewing" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Required readings and viewing&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The Computer, Enhance! entries require a subscription. Link to them; do not copy their paid content, homework listings or reference decoder into course materials. The course&#39;s subset, output format, test listings and decoder are its own. Links and durations were checked on 2026-09-22 against the <a href="https://www.computerenhance.com/p/table-of-contents" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">official table of contents</a>; the implementing agent re-verifies every link on the day of writing.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Resource</th><th>Assigned portion</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href="https://www.computerenhance.com/p/instruction-decoding-on-the-8086" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">CE: Instruction Decoding on the 8086</a></td><td>Full episode, 28:28</td><td>Registers, the register-to-register <code>mov</code> encoding, and the <code>d</code> and <code>w</code> bits.</td></tr><tr><td><a href="https://www.computerenhance.com/p/decoding-multiple-instructions-and" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">CE: Decoding Multiple Instructions and Suffixes</a></td><td>Full episode, 43:51</td><td>Variable-length instructions, immediates, and the <code>mod</code> field. Memory modes are watched now for context and implemented in week 7.</td></tr><tr><td><a href="https://www.computerenhance.com/p/opcode-patterns-in-8086-arithmetic" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">CE: Opcode Patterns in 8086 Arithmetic</a></td><td>Full episode, 20:01</td><td>The shared encoding patterns of <code>add</code>, <code>sub</code> and <code>cmp</code>. The conditional jumps it also covers are the optional stretch exercise.</td></tr><tr><td><a href="http://www.bitsavers.org/components/intel/8086/9800722-03_The_8086_Family_Users_Manual_Oct79.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Intel, The 8086 Family User&#39;s Manual (October 1979), via bitsavers</a></td><td>Chapter 4&#39;s instruction-set encoding table and machine-instruction decoding guide for <code>MOV</code>, <code>ADD</code>, <code>SUB</code> and <code>CMP</code>, and the register-field tables (the implementing agent records the exact table numbers and page numbers after checking the scan)</td><td>The primary technical reference for every encoding in the subset. The course&#39;s own table is checked against it entry by entry.</td></tr><tr><td><a href="https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg14/www/docs/n1570.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">WG14 public C11 draft N1570</a></td><td>§6.2.6.1 (object representations; byte order is not specified), §6.3.1.1 (integer promotions), §6.3.1.3 (conversions to signed types), §6.5.7 (shifts), §7.20.1.1 (exact-width types), §7.21.7.1 and 7.21.10 (<code>fgetc</code>, <code>feof</code>, <code>ferror</code>)</td><td>Primary authority for the C side of decoding.</td></tr><tr><td><a href="https://sourceware.org/binutils/docs/binutils/objdump.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">GNU binutils: objdump</a></td><td><code>-D</code>, <code>-b binary</code>, <code>-m i8086</code>, <code>-M intel</code></td><td>The toolchain cross-check used by the supplied tests.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Required viewing is about <strong>92 minutes</strong> (28:28 + 43:51 + 20:01); focused work with the manual&#39;s encoding tables and the C11 clauses brings the total to about <strong>125 minutes</strong>. The rest of the week is for implementation and explanation.</p><h2 id="the-course-subset-and-output-format-definitions-used-by-all-exercises" tabindex="-1">The course subset and output format (definitions used by all exercises) <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-course-subset-and-output-format-definitions-used-by-all-exercises" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The course subset and output format (definitions used by all exercises)&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Instructions in the subset</strong> (all others are <code>DEC_UNSUPPORTED_OPCODE</code>): <ul><li><code>mov</code> register to register: <code>100010dw</code> then ModR/M (<code>0x88</code>–<code>0x8B</code>).</li><li><code>mov</code> immediate to register: <code>1011wrrr</code> then an 8-bit (<code>w = 0</code>) or 16-bit (<code>w = 1</code>) immediate (<code>0xB0</code>–<code>0xBF</code>).</li><li><code>add</code>, <code>sub</code>, <code>cmp</code> register with register: <code>00ooo0dw</code> then ModR/M, where <code>ooo</code> is <code>000</code> (<code>add</code>, <code>0x00</code>–<code>0x03</code>), <code>101</code> (<code>sub</code>, <code>0x28</code>–<code>0x2B</code>) or <code>111</code> (<code>cmp</code>, <code>0x38</code>–<code>0x3B</code>).</li><li><code>add</code>, <code>sub</code>, <code>cmp</code> immediate to register: <code>100000sw</code> then ModR/M whose <code>reg</code> field is <code>000</code>, <code>101</code> or <code>111</code>, then the immediate: 16 bits when <code>s:w = 01</code>, 8 bits otherwise, sign-extended to 16 bits when <code>s:w = 11</code> (<code>0x80</code>, <code>0x81</code>, <code>0x83</code>).</li><li><code>add</code>, <code>sub</code>, <code>cmp</code> immediate to accumulator: <code>00ooo10w</code> then an 8- or 16-bit immediate (<code>0x04</code>/<code>0x05</code>, <code>0x2C</code>/<code>0x2D</code>, <code>0x3C</code>/<code>0x3D</code>).</li></ul></li><li><strong>Outside the subset this week, with explicit statuses:</strong> a ModR/M <code>mod</code> other than <code>11</code> in a recognized form is <code>DEC_UNSUPPORTED_MODE</code> (week 7); a <code>reg</code> field in <code>0x80</code>–<code>0x83</code> other than <code>000</code>, <code>101</code> or <code>111</code> (<code>or</code>, <code>adc</code>, <code>sbb</code>, <code>and</code>, <code>xor</code>) is <code>DEC_UNSUPPORTED_OPERATION</code>; opcode <code>0x82</code> (<code>s:w = 10</code>) is <code>DEC_UNSUPPORTED_OPCODE</code>. <code>objdump</code> decodes <code>82 c1 05</code> as <code>add cl,0x5</code> (checked on the reference machine), so the course&#39;s rejection is a <em>scope decision</em>, not a claim about the hardware; the implementing agent records what the 1979 manual says about this encoding and the answer key explains the decision.</li><li><strong>Status order</strong> when decoding one instruction: read the first byte (none: <code>DEC_TRUNCATED</code>); if its opcode is not in the subset, <code>DEC_UNSUPPORTED_OPCODE</code>; if the form needs a ModR/M byte and none remains, <code>DEC_TRUNCATED</code>; then <code>DEC_UNSUPPORTED_MODE</code>, then <code>DEC_UNSUPPORTED_OPERATION</code>; finally, if the immediate&#39;s bytes are not all present, <code>DEC_TRUNCATED</code>. Each failure reports the offset of the instruction&#39;s first byte. <code>DEC_OUTPUT_TOO_SMALL</code> and <code>DEC_INVALID_ARGUMENT</code> are stream/format API failures, not byte-encoding classifications; file I/O and size errors are command-level errors rather than decoder statuses.</li><li><strong>Canonical text:</strong> lowercase mnemonic, one space, destination, <code>&quot;, &quot;</code>, source: <code>mov cx, bx</code>, <code>add si, -2</code>, <code>cmp al, 12</code>. Registers: <code>w = 0</code> → <code>al cl dl bl ah ch dh bh</code>; <code>w = 1</code> → <code>ax cx dx bx sp bp si di</code> (field values 0–7). The <code>d</code> bit selects which of <code>reg</code> and <code>r/m</code> is the destination (<code>d = 1</code>: <code>reg</code> is the destination). Immediates are printed in signed decimal <strong>as a value of the operand&#39;s width</strong>: the 16-bit pattern <code>0xFFF4</code> prints as <code>-12</code>, the 8-bit pattern <code>0xF4</code> in <code>mov cl, …</code> prints as <code>-12</code>, and an 8-bit immediate sign-extended by <code>s = 1</code> prints its extended value. The text contains no size keyword because every operand in the subset is a register whose width fixes the size (week 7 adds <code>byte</code> and <code>word</code>).</li><li><strong>Stream output:</strong> the first line is <code>bits 16</code>, then one instruction per line, each ending with <code>\\n</code>. The output is NASM-compatible so that learners who have an assembler can reassemble it; the course does not require one.</li></ul><h2 id="deliverable-and-working-environment" tabindex="-1">Deliverable and working environment <a class="header-anchor" href="#deliverable-and-working-environment" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Deliverable and working environment&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The single graded assignment is an <strong>8086 subset decoder notebook</strong>: seven exercises that build a decoder from bit fields to a byte-stream command, their tests, and a report whose central content is <em>hand-decoding before running</em>. The implementation provides a learner package with prompts, typed warning-clean TODO functions and supplied drivers; complete reference C, the full written answer key and an exemplar report live in a separate instructor package. As in earlier weeks, the answer key answers every learner-facing “explain,” “predict,” “derive,” “compare,” and “interpret” request as well as every coding task.</p><ul><li>Reference environment: x86-64 Linux (or Ubuntu under WSL), GCC and Clang, C11, <code>make</code>, Python 3 for the harness, and binutils <code>objdump</code> built with <code>i8086</code> support (present on the reference WSL image; the cross-check is skipped with a labelled message where it is not). No assembler is required; NASM is an optional, gated extra.</li><li>Builds: <code>-std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Werror -O0</code> and the same at <code>-O2</code>, plus AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer on both compilers. Add <code>-Wconversion -Wsign-conversion</code> to the reference solution&#39;s build (not required of learners), because this week is about exact integer conversions.</li><li>E01–E05 are standalone <code>exNN.c</code> files with supplied drivers, included by the public C harness with <code>main</code> renamed, as in weeks 1–4. E06 and E07 form the <code>decode8086</code> program from three translation units (<code>decode.h</code>, <code>decode.c</code>, <code>decode8086.c</code>). Supplied <code>support/decode_types.h</code> defines the types below and no exercise answer: <ul><li><code>typedef enum { DEC_OK = 0, DEC_TRUNCATED, DEC_UNSUPPORTED_OPCODE, DEC_UNSUPPORTED_MODE, DEC_UNSUPPORTED_OPERATION, DEC_OUTPUT_TOO_SMALL, DEC_INVALID_ARGUMENT } DecodeStatus;</code></li><li><code>typedef enum { OP_MOV, OP_ADD, OP_SUB, OP_CMP } Operation;</code></li><li><code>typedef enum { OPERAND_REG, OPERAND_IMM } OperandKind;</code></li><li><code>typedef struct { OperandKind kind; unsigned wide; unsigned reg; int32_t imm; } Operand;</code> (<code>imm</code> holds the operand-width signed value)</li><li><code>typedef struct { Operation op; Operand dst; Operand src; unsigned length; } Instruction;</code></li><li><code>#define DEC_MAX_INPUT 65536u</code> (one 8086 segment; larger files are rejected) and <code>#define DEC_MAX_TEXT 32u</code>.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Independent oracles (supplied).</strong> <code>tests/oracle8086.py</code> builds its expected table in the <strong>encoding</strong> direction: for every instruction form in the subset, every register choice and every <code>d</code> value, and a fixed set of immediates (all 256 8-bit values; 0, 1, 0x7F, 0x80, 0xFF, 0x100, 0x7FFF, 0x8000, 0xFFFF and a deterministic sample for 16-bit), it writes the bytes and the canonical text from the course table. For negative cases it uses a separate, documented opcode/form classification and the status order above; merely failing to find a generated encoding does not distinguish unsupported, truncated and valid but unsampled 16-bit immediates. The table itself is checked against the 1979 manual, entry by entry, in the validation record. The <strong><code>objdump</code> cross-check</strong> disassembles every oracle byte sequence with <code>-b binary -m i8086 -M intel</code> and compares mnemonic, registers and immediate after normalizing <code>objdump</code>&#39;s unsigned hexadecimal immediates to the operand-width signed value; it is a toolchain observation, labelled and gated, and it excludes <code>0x82</code> by design.</li><li>Keep executable examples defined: read bytes as <code>unsigned char</code> or <code>uint8_t</code>, never plain <code>char</code>; assemble 16-bit values as <code>(uint16_t)(lo | (hi &lt;&lt; 8))</code> from promoted values that cannot overflow <code>int</code>; convert to a signed value by arithmetic (<code>v &gt;= 0x8000 ? (int32_t)v - 0x10000 : (int32_t)v</code>), never by a narrowing cast; no <code>memcpy</code> of two bytes into a <code>uint16_t</code> (the result would depend on the host&#39;s byte order, §6.2.6.1); no right shift of a negative value; every array index is checked against its length before use.</li><li>The playground is <code>decode8086</code> on hand-written byte files: write the bytes with <code>printf &#39;\\x89\\xd9&#39; &gt; t.bin</code> or the supplied <code>tools/hex2bin.py</code>, predict the text, run, and reconcile. It feeds the notebook rather than adding a second graded assignment.</li><li>Learner submission: source files, the build file, test output, the learner test cases (E07) and <code>observations.md</code>.</li></ul><h2 id="the-7-micro-exercises" tabindex="-1">The 7 micro-exercises <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-7-micro-exercises" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The 7 micro-exercises&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Each item needs a learner prompt, a compilable typed TODO starter, at least one success test and one boundary/error test where applicable, a complete C reference solution, and a <strong>substantive worked explanation</strong> of the mechanism and every associated question. Functions that write results leave their outputs unchanged on failure; “rejected” means the function returns 0 (or a non-OK status) and writes nothing.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Task to implement</th><th>Required observable result and explanation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>01 — Bit fields</strong></td><td><code>typedef struct { unsigned mod, reg, rm; } ModRM;</code> Implement <code>ModRM split_modrm(uint8_t byte)</code> (bits 7–6, 5–3, 2–0) and <code>unsigned bit_at(uint8_t byte, unsigned index)</code> (rejects <code>index &gt; 7</code> by returning 0 and is documented as such), using only unsigned shifts and masks. The driver prints the fields of <code>0xD9</code>, <code>0xCB</code>, <code>0xC6</code> and <code>0x00</code> in binary.</td><td>Tests: all 256 bytes, each field compared with the oracle&#39;s field table, and reassembly \`mod &lt;&lt; 6</td></tr><tr><td><strong>02 — Register names</strong></td><td><code>const char *reg_name(unsigned wide, unsigned reg)</code> returns the canonical name, or NULL when <code>wide &gt; 1</code> or <code>reg &gt; 7</code>. The driver prints both tables.</td><td>Tests: all 16 valid combinations and rejected out-of-range values. Explain that <code>al</code>/<code>ah</code> are the low and high halves of <code>ax</code> (and likewise for <code>cx</code>, <code>dx</code>, <code>bx</code>), so field value 4 means <code>ah</code> when <code>w = 0</code> but <code>sp</code> when <code>w = 1</code>; that <code>sp</code>, <code>bp</code>, <code>si</code> and <code>di</code> have no 8-bit halves on the 8086; and, as a labelled ISA comparison to be checked in the Intel SDM, what x86-64 changed (REX prefixes, <code>spl</code>/<code>sil</code>, 64-bit registers) without implementing it.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>03 — Immediates and signs</strong></td><td><code>int read_imm(const uint8_t *bytes, size_t avail, size_t offset, unsigned size, uint16_t *out)</code> reads 1 or 2 bytes little-endian (rejects <code>size</code> other than 1 or 2, NULL pointers, and any read past <code>avail</code>, using <code>offset &lt;= avail &amp;&amp; size &lt;= avail - offset</code> so the addition is never evaluated before the check); <code>int32_t to_signed(uint16_t value, unsigned wide)</code> returns the signed value of an 8- or 16-bit pattern, with the documented precondition <code>wide</code> is 0 or 1 and, for <code>wide == 0</code>, <code>value &lt;= 0xFF</code>; <code>uint16_t sign_extend8(uint8_t value)</code>.</td><td>Tests: <code>34 12</code> reads as <code>0x1234</code>; every 8-bit value&#39;s <code>to_signed</code> and <code>sign_extend8</code>; the 16-bit boundaries <code>0x7FFF</code>, <code>0x8000</code>, <code>0xFFFF</code>; reads at the last valid offset and one past it; <code>offset</code> near <code>SIZE_MAX</code>; outputs unchanged on rejection. Explain little-endian order as a property of the 8086&#39;s encoding (not of the host), why assembling from bytes is host-independent while <code>memcpy</code> into a <code>uint16_t</code> is not, why the narrowing cast is implementation-defined (C11 §6.3.1.3 ¶3) and the arithmetic form is not, and two&#39;s-complement sign extension with a worked <code>0xFE → 0xFFFE → −2</code>.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>04 — Decoding one instruction</strong></td><td><code>DecodeStatus decode_one(const uint8_t *bytes, size_t avail, Instruction *out)</code> for the subset, following the status order above and setting <code>length</code>. NULL <code>out</code>, or NULL <code>bytes</code> with nonzero <code>avail</code>, returns <code>DEC_INVALID_ARGUMENT</code> without writing; <code>*out</code> is unchanged on any failure.</td><td>Tests: the full oracle table; <strong>every</strong> two-byte prefix <code>(b0, b1)</code> in 0–255 × 0–255 with each of 0–2 following bytes, compared with the oracle for status, length and decoded fields; <code>0x82</code>, <code>0x80</code> with <code>reg = 001</code> (<code>or</code>), <code>0x88</code> with <code>mod = 00</code>, and each truncation point; sign-extension cases <code>83 C6 FE</code> (<code>add si, -2</code>) and <code>80 C1 FE</code> (<code>add cl, -2</code>); the equivalent encodings <code>89 D9</code> and <code>8B CB</code> both decode to <code>mov cx, bx</code>. Explain the three <code>add</code>/<code>sub</code>/<code>cmp</code> forms and the shared 3-bit operation code (bits 5–3 of the first byte in the register and accumulator forms, the <code>reg</code> field in <code>0x80</code>–<code>0x83</code>), which the answer key tabulates and checks against the manual; the roles of <code>d</code>, <code>w</code> and <code>s</code>; the status order and why an ordered list makes diagnostics deterministic (week 4); the exhaustive check of how far “length depends only on the first byte” holds (true for accepted instructions in this subset; false for the status; false for lengths once week 7&#39;s memory modes are admitted); and a faulty decoder that reads the immediate before checking <code>avail</code>.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>05 — Canonical text</strong></td><td><code>int format_instruction(const Instruction *ins, char *out, size_t size)</code> writes the canonical text and a NUL, rejecting a NULL pointer, an invalid field, or <code>size</code> smaller than the text length plus one. Validate the complete instruction, including operand kinds, register indices, widths and signed immediate ranges; format into a local <code>DEC_MAX_TEXT</code> buffer, check <code>snprintf</code>&#39;s result, then copy the complete string only when it fits the caller&#39;s buffer.</td><td>Tests: every oracle entry&#39;s text exactly; the longest possible text in the subset (the answer derives it, for example <code>cmp ax, -32768</code>, and shows it fits <code>DEC_MAX_TEXT</code>); the buffer boundary at that length and one byte less; sentinel-filled outputs unchanged on every rejection. Explain why decoding is a function but assembling is not (<code>89 D9</code> and <code>8B CB</code>; <code>81 C6 FE FF</code> and <code>83 C6 FE</code> both print <code>add si, -2</code>), so a text round trip cannot recover the bytes; why immediates are printed as signed width values (and what an unsigned choice, as <code>objdump</code> makes, would change); and why an integer conversion in <code>snprintf</code> does not depend on the locale&#39;s decimal point.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>06 — The stream decoder</strong></td><td>In <code>decode.c</code>: <code>DecodeStatus decode_stream(const uint8_t *bytes, size_t n, char *text, size_t text_size, size_t *text_len, size_t *error_offset)</code> decodes every instruction and writes the complete output (<code>bits 16</code> and one line per instruction) only if the whole stream decodes and fits. Treat NULL <code>bytes</code> as valid only when <code>n == 0</code>; require non-NULL output pointers. First validate and count the text bytes with checked <code>size_t</code> arithmetic, including the terminator; only then write. On a byte-decoding failure, leave <code>text</code> and <code>text_len</code> unchanged and set <code>*error_offset</code> to the offending instruction&#39;s start. On <code>DEC_OUTPUT_TOO_SMALL</code> or <code>DEC_INVALID_ARGUMENT</code>, leave all caller outputs unchanged (where non-NULL); on success set <code>*text_len</code> to the number of visible bytes and <code>*error_offset</code> to <code>n</code>. In <code>decode8086.c</code> (supplied argument handling, learner file reading): read the file named by the single argument with <code>fgetc</code>, distinguishing end of file from <code>ferror</code>, rejecting files larger than <code>DEC_MAX_INPUT</code> and failures of <code>fclose</code>; print <code>decode8086: input error</code>, <code>decode8086: input too large</code>, or <code>decode8086: output error</code> for command-level failures, and <code>decode8086: STATUS at offset N</code> for byte-decoding failures. All errors exit 1. Standard output is empty on read, decode and format failure; a failed output write may leave a partial stream and must be reported.</td><td>Tests: the supplied listing files (original to this course, written for the subset) decode to their expected text; an empty file prints <code>bits 16</code> alone; a file of exactly <code>DEC_MAX_INPUT</code> bytes of valid two-byte instructions succeeds (the test sizes <code>text</code> from the derived maximum output length) and one byte more is rejected; a truncated final instruction reports the offset of its first byte; an unsupported instruction in the middle produces no standard output; a too-small text buffer preserves its sentinel and output parameters; a missing file and a directory are rejected; fault injection makes <code>fgetc</code>/<code>ferror</code>/<code>fclose</code> fail in the test translation unit only; sanitizer runs report no leak. Explain why an instruction stream must be decoded from its start (the decoder cannot resynchronize at an arbitrary offset, shown with a stream that decodes differently from offset 1), validate-then-commit for text output, and the bounded read.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>07 — Hand-decoding and the test oracle</strong></td><td>Before running anything, hand-decode five supplied byte sequences (with at least one sign-extended immediate, one <code>d = 0</code>/<code>d = 1</code> pair and one unsupported instruction) into text and length on success, or status and offending offset on failure, showing each applicable bit field. Then add at least <strong>eight</strong> purposeful cases to <code>tests/learner_cases.txt</code> (one line each: hexadecimal bytes, expected text or status and offset, and a one-line reason); the supplied runner executes them against the decoder and the oracle and fails when either disagrees.</td><td>The answer supplies the five hand decodings with the fields shown, an exemplar set of eight cases with reasons, and a trace of one case through the oracle&#39;s encoding-direction construction. It explains why the oracle is independent of the decoder (it never decodes; it enumerates encodings from a table checked against the manual), why the <code>objdump</code> cross-check is a second, toolchain-level witness with different conventions (unsigned hexadecimal immediates, acceptance of <code>0x82</code>), and what neither can catch (a misreading of the manual shared by the course table and the decoder).</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="practice-problems-and-stretch-work" tabindex="-1">Practice problems and stretch work <a class="header-anchor" href="#practice-problems-and-stretch-work" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Practice problems and stretch work&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The package includes these <strong>six ungraded practice problems</strong>, with answer keys separate from the learner prompt:</p><ol><li>Classify five statements as ISO C guarantees, implementation-defined behavior, 8086 ISA facts, or false in general, and justify each: <code>(int16_t)0xFFF4</code> is −12 on every C implementation; <code>uint8_t</code> exists on every C implementation; the 8086 stores the low byte of a 16-bit immediate first; <code>(b0 | (b1 &lt;&lt; 8))</code> with <code>uint8_t</code> operands depends on the host&#39;s byte order; the 8086 register field value 4 always names <code>sp</code>.</li><li>Hand-decode five supplied sequences in the subset, showing the opcode bits, <code>d</code>/<code>w</code>/<code>s</code>, <code>mod</code>/<code>reg</code>/<code>r/m</code>, the immediate bytes and the length, then write the canonical text. (This is the syllabus&#39;s “hand-decode five byte sequences and compare” extension; compare with <code>decode8086</code> afterwards.)</li><li>Encode by hand: give every encoding in the subset of <code>mov ax, bx</code>, <code>add cl, 5</code>, <code>cmp si, -1</code> and <code>sub al, 200</code> (if <code>200</code> is not representable as the operand&#39;s signed value, say what the assembler text must be instead), and state which is shortest.</li><li>Decode two memory-mode ModR/M bytes from the second reading (<code>mod = 00</code> and <code>mod = 01</code>) as far as the fields go, and state what information week 7 must add; say why this week&#39;s decoder must reject them rather than guess their length.</li><li>Explain, with one byte stream, why decoding from offset 1 instead of offset 0 can produce a different but valid-looking instruction sequence, and what this implies for disassemblers that start in the middle of code.</li><li>Run <code>objdump -d</code> on week 5&#39;s <code>geo.o</code> (x86-64), find a <code>mov</code> with opcode <code>0x89</code> or <code>0x8B</code> if that build contains one, and identify its ModR/M byte and any REX prefix. If neither opcode occurs, record that observation and disassemble a supplied three-byte x86-64 binary fixture (<code>48 89 D8</code>, <code>mov rax, rbx</code>) with <code>objdump -D -b binary -m i386:x86-64 -M intel</code> for the same byte-field analysis; label x86-64 ISA facts (cite the Intel SDM), and call a finding a compiler-output observation only when it came from <code>geo.o</code>. Different valid disassemblies are accepted.</li></ol><p>Include two optional stretch exercises: <strong>(S01) conditional jumps and loops</strong>: extend <code>decode_one</code> to the twenty short-branch opcodes (<code>0x70</code>–<code>0x7F</code>, <code>0xE0</code>–<code>0xE3</code>) with a signed 8-bit displacement, printed as <code>jnz $+N</code> relative to the start of the instruction (so <code>75 FC</code> prints <code>jnz $-2</code>; the answer derives the conversion from the displacement, which is relative to the <em>next</em> instruction); state which mnemonic aliases the course prints, and test against the oracle&#39;s extended table and <code>objdump</code>. <strong>(S02) a decoding table</strong>: replace the E04 branch structure with a 256-entry table indexed by the first byte (form, operation, width source), compare the two decoders exhaustively on all two-byte prefixes and all 8-bit immediate values, then either compare all 65,536 16-bit immediate values for each applicable form or give a reasoned proof that both use identical byte assembly, sign conversion and formatting for the full 16-bit domain. Discuss the trade-off between the manual&#39;s bit patterns and a table without making a speed claim. A two-byte-prefix test alone does not prove equivalence for later immediate bytes.</p><h2 id="required-answer-key-coverage" tabindex="-1">Required answer key coverage <a class="header-anchor" href="#required-answer-key-coverage" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Required answer key coverage&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Assign stable IDs to every subquestion in the learner materials and use the same IDs in the instructor answer key (<code>E01.C</code>–<code>E07.C</code>, <code>E01.Q</code>–<code>E07.Q</code>, <code>P01</code>–<code>P06</code>, <code>S01</code>–<code>S02</code>, <code>R01</code>–<code>R05</code>). The answer key contains annotated working C for each exercise <strong>and</strong> the following written explanations. A one-line result, test output, or “see source code” does not count. Explanations walk through the reasoning with a concrete byte sequence, the relevant edge case, a plausible faulty approach where the item calls for one, and the portability boundary.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Exercise</th><th>Written solution must cover</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>01</strong></td><td>The ModR/M fields and their roles in the subset; shifts and masks on promoted values; the reassembly identity; the faulty unmasked shift.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>02</strong></td><td>Register numbering, the 8-bit halves, the <code>w</code> bit, and the labelled x86-64 comparison.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>03</strong></td><td>Little-endian encoding versus host byte order; the implementation-defined narrowing conversion and its defined replacement; sign extension worked in binary; the overflow-checked bounds test.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>04</strong></td><td>Each form with its bit pattern checked against the manual (table and page cited); the shared operation code; <code>d</code>, <code>w</code>, <code>s</code>; the status order; the scope of the first-byte length claim; the <code>0x82</code> scope decision; a faulty read-before-check.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>05</strong></td><td>The canonical text and the longest-text derivation; decoding as a function and assembling as a relation, with two worked pairs of encodings; signed versus unsigned immediate printing.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>06</strong></td><td>Sequential decoding and why resynchronization fails; validate-then-commit; bounded reading with <code>EOF</code> versus <code>ferror</code>; the offset reported for each error.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>07</strong></td><td>The five hand decodings; the exemplar cases and their reasons; the oracle trace; the independence and limits of both oracles.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Provide <strong>fully worked answers to all six practice problems and both optional stretch exercises</strong>, including runnable code for both stretch tasks and clearly labelled toolchain output, while accepting different valid <code>objdump</code> versions and compiler output. Provide a completed exemplar <code>observations.md</code> that answers every report prompt:</p><ul><li><strong>R01</strong> the build environment, including compiler, binutils and Python versions and the flags used;</li><li><strong>R02</strong> a prediction, observation and explanation row for each of E01–E07, with the E07 hand decodings and the E05 longest-text prediction recorded before running;</li><li><strong>R03</strong> a claim ledger labelling at least eight statements as C11, implementation-defined, 8086 ISA (manual), x86-64 ISA (SDM), toolchain (<code>objdump</code>), oracle, or observed;</li><li><strong>R04</strong> a comparison of the decoder, the oracle and <code>objdump</code> on three chosen sequences, including one where <code>objdump</code>&#39;s text differs from the canonical text and why the difference is a convention rather than an error;</li><li><strong>R05</strong> the submission inventory, one durable source → mechanism → observation defense (from a C expression to the bits the decoder reads), and one open question for week 7 (for example, how the decoder should learn the length of an instruction with a memory operand).</li></ul><p>Include a prompt-to-answer checklist covering all <strong>27</strong> IDs (14 exercise, 6 practice, 2 stretch, 5 report).</p><h2 id="assessment-and-acceptance-criteria" tabindex="-1">Assessment and acceptance criteria <a class="header-anchor" href="#assessment-and-acceptance-criteria" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Assessment and acceptance criteria&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Functional correctness (40%):</strong> All seven exercises compile and pass the deterministic tests under GCC and Clang; statuses, offsets and texts are exact. Points: E01 4, E02 3, E03 6, E04 12, E05 5, E06 7, E07 3.</li><li><strong>Encoding and C reasoning (25%):</strong> Five points each for bit fields and register selection; immediates, byte order and signed conversion; forms, operation codes and the status order; decoding versus assembling; and oracle independence and its limits.</li><li><strong>Prediction and evidence (20%):</strong> Five points each for hand decodings recorded before running; the claim ledger&#39;s labelling; the three-way comparison; and correct citation of the manual and the C11 clauses.</li><li><strong>Clarity (15%):</strong> Source, prompts, test failures, and the report can be understood without the videos open beside them.</li></ul><p>There is no timing and no performance threshold this week.</p><p>Acceptance checks for the agent implementing this week: every exercise has learner instructions, a tested reference C solution, and a full written answer to every question; all practice problems, both stretch exercises and every <code>observations.md</code> prompt have worked answers; an inventory script confirms the exact set of 27 IDs; strict builds pass with both compilers at <code>-O0</code> and <code>-O2</code> (and the reference with <code>-Wconversion -Wsign-conversion</code>), and the reference solutions pass under AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer with no leak on any error path; <strong>every entry of the course&#39;s encoding table is checked against the 1979 manual, with the table and page recorded</strong>; <strong>the oracle builds its expectations in the encoding direction and shares no code with the decoder</strong>; <strong>the exhaustive two-byte-prefix comparison runs in every configuration</strong>; <strong>the <code>objdump</code> cross-check runs where <code>i8086</code> support exists and is skipped with a labelled message elsewhere</strong>; <strong>no reference code uses an implementation-defined narrowing conversion or a host-byte-order <code>memcpy</code></strong>; <strong>every structural claim in the answer key (lengths, the shared operation code, the longest text) is checked exhaustively or by a counterexample search before it is published</strong>; outputs are unchanged on rejection; the learner scaffolds compile warning-clean and fail <code>make test</code> for unimplemented behavior rather than a build or harness error; mutation testing of the harness has been run and its survivors classified in the validation record; every reading link is re-verified.</p><p>The core target is 600 minutes: 125 for viewing and manual consultation, 15 for setup, 315 for E01–E07 (30, 25, 40, 80, 45, 55, 40), 55 for the report, 45 for practice, and 45 for debugging. The playground is included in E07 time. Optional stretch work is additional. This is an <strong>unpiloted</strong> estimate for experienced C programmers using the supplied scaffolds and oracle, not a demonstrated completion time, and the validation record must say so. If a pilot shows the week is too long, the first reduction to review is supplying the <code>decode8086.c</code> file-reading code (as week 4 supplied its argument parsing), which removes plumbing without removing any decoding or reasoning.</p><p><strong>Pilot procedure (pending):</strong> an experienced C programmer who has not seen the answer key attempts the learner package, logs reading, implementation, testing and writing time per exercise, and records every point at which help was needed; the results revise this budget in a later correction. Do not mark the pilot complete without participant data.</p></div>`);
}
